// rest api //


GET -> read and list the requested data
PUT -> replace entirely with new data (change all field)
PATCH -> replace only few fields (partial update)
POST -> add completly new dataset (don't require url)
DELETE -> delete the data from data base

idempotency -> A method is idempotent if sending the same request many times leaves the server in the same state as sending it once.
                 (POST can't be idempotent since it can cause dublication of data in database, somtimes PATCH can too)
              
           -> "This is why payment APIs often ask for an “idempotency key” on POST requests, so a retry doesn’t charge the customer twice"
  
              # That’s the full lifecycle of an idempotency key:
                 1.The client generates a UUID (unique key) per logical operation and reuses it on retries.
                 2.The server atomically claims the key, which prevents races.
                 3.It processes the request and stores the response together with the key.
                 4.A repeat of the same key returns the stored response, and a repeat with a different body is rejected.
                 5.Stale “in progress” keys expire, and the work and the key commit together.


genral statuscode in use:
            200->ok ,201->sucess,400->not found,401->unauthorized,404->forbiden

